# R8: deterministic checkout bytes for security-pinned PowerShell evidence.
#
# WHY. These files are covered by SHA-256 identity claims: the trusted-parser identity pin
# (Test-SjpTrustedParserIdentity), the lifecycle helper resolver, the layer-0 validator's pin over
# scripts/lib/RuntimeLogScan.ps1, the checkpoint byte binding, and the validated source bytes shipped for
# independent review.
#
# The repository previously declared no attributes at all, so end-of-line handling was decided entirely by
# each user's core.autocrlf. Measured against the R7 commit, every file matched by the rules below hashed differently
# depending on that setting: with core.autocrlf=false the checkout equalled the blob (LF), while with
# core.autocrlf=true - the default on a Windows install - the checkout was CRLF and matched nothing that had
# been pinned. Concretely, the layer-0 validator pins RuntimeLogScan.ps1 to E686C072..., and a default-Windows
# checkout of that same commit produced F3C1B7E9..., so the pin failed on bytes Git itself reported as
# unmodified. An identity control whose outcome depends on a personal Git setting is not authenticating the
# artifact.
#
# eol=lf rather than -text: -text would also give byte stability, but it marks the files binary and Git would
# stop producing readable diffs for exactly the sources an independent reviewer needs to read. eol=lf keeps
# them diffable while making the checked-out bytes byte-for-byte equal to the canonical blob under any
# core.autocrlf value.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. A repository-wide rule would renormalise unrelated application files and drag
# them into this security checkpoint's delta. Only the security-pinned population is covered here; every blob
# below is already LF, so this rule introduces no renormalisation churn.
# The attribute file governs ITSELF. Without this line .gitattributes is the one path in the security
# population whose checked-out bytes still depend on core.autocrlf: a default Windows clone materialises it
# CRLF (BA598FC3..., 1952 bytes) while the canonical blob is LF (CA41D043..., 1925 bytes). It ships as a
# validated source carrying a SHA-256 claim and is byte-bound by the checkpoint, so the file that makes every
# other hash reproducible was the one hash a reviewer could not reproduce from a stock clone.
.gitattributes          text eol=lf
scripts/lib/*.ps1       text eol=lf
scripts/tests/*.ps1     text eol=lf
scripts/validate-*.ps1  text eol=lf

# functions/src/lib/moneyLog.ts joins the R8 checkpoint as a REQUIRED source: it is matched by .gitignore:12
# ('lib/', written for the compiled functions/lib output), was never committed to any ref, and two TRACKED
# sources inside the IAM import graph import it - so a clean checkout of the checkpoint did not compile at
# all (jest ran 0 of 53 tests with TS2307). Being in the checkpoint, its bytes are now SHA-256 pinned by the
# checkpoint byte binding and enumerated in the battery's source manifest, which puts it in exactly the
# position that motivated this file: without an eol rule a stock Windows clone would materialise it CRLF and
# reproduce neither hash.
functions/src/lib/moneyLog.ts  text eol=lf
